[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608203000-i64-shared-and-the-stdlib-that-already-existed"
  :adr/title "kotoba-lang/i64, and the foundational stdlib that already existed"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-20"
  :adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner asked for the JVM dependency to be removed, then for `date`, `min`\nand \"everything else amounting to a basic library\" to be designed and\nimplemented in the kotoba-lang org.\n\nMeasuring before creating changed the answer.\n\n## What was measured (2026-08-20)\n\n**Where the JVM dependency actually is.** Across the five backends (\u7dbe), the\nsource is already portable and the VERIFICATION is not: 70 of 70 test files\nare `.clj`, zero are `.cljc`. `kotoba-kir` is the sharpest case -- 9 of 9\nsource files are `.cljc` and it ships a `cljs_i64.cljs`, while all 31 of its\ntest files are JVM-only.\n\n**Which host primitives are actually reached for**, counted with tightened\npatterns after a first pass inflated `format` by matching the `:format`\nkeyword:\n\n| primitive | hits | repos |\n|---|---|---|\n| `java.nio` | 547 | kotoba-component 296, amu 183 |\n| `thrown? clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo` | 193 | amu 110, kototama 29 |\n| `js/BigInt` | 187 | amu 92, kotoba-native 43, kotoba-kir 42 |\n| `StandardCharsets` | 86 | kotoba-component 27, amu 26 |\n| `Long/MIN_VALUE` / `MAX_VALUE` | 79 | amu 29, kotoba-kir 28, kotoba-native 17 |\n| `(format ...)` | 57 | kototama 21, kotoba-component 19 |\n| `Math/*` | 50 | amu 30, kotoba-kir 20 |\n| `MessageDigest` | 47 | amu 15, aiueos 13 |\n\n## The finding that changed the work\n\n**The foundational stdlib already exists.** `ADR-kotoba-lang-foundational-stdlib`\nis accepted and implemented: 12 foundational plus 3 composite libraries shipped\nat v0.1.0 / M6 on 2026-07-01, layered data / cap / IO / tooling, with exactly\nthe design rules the owner asked for -- zero third-party runtime deps, every\nnamespace `.cljc`, capability-parameterized rather than direct-OS, runs on\nkotoba-WASM.\n\nAll 17 repositories exist and are live: coll, spec, json, wit, async, time,\nfs, http, io, test, fmt, lsp, scheduler, store, lint, plus num and bytes.\n\nSo the primitives named in the request are already owned:\n\n- `date` -> `kotoba-lang/time`, which states it holds no `System/currentTimeMillis`\n  and takes wall-clock from an injected clock capability\n- bytes -> `kotoba-lang/bytes`, whose contract is that every bytes value is a\n  plain `vector<int 0..255>`, never a platform byte-array\n- digest -> `kotoba-lang/hash` and `org-nist-sha2`\n\n**Creating `date` or `min` would have put a second answer on the same plane.**\nThe request is satisfied by adoption, not creation.\n\n## Decision\n\n**Create exactly one library: `kotoba-lang/i64`.**\n\nIt is the one measured gap with no owner. `num` is GPU/BLAS linear algebra and\ndoes not carry integer limits; `i64`, `int`, `integer`, `math` and `limits` did\nnot exist. The semantics were present but private -- `kotoba.kir.cljs-i64` had\nthe ClojureScript half and no JVM half, so nothing outside kotoba-kir could use\nit, and four repositories each spelled it themselves.\n\nThe library is `.cljc`, zero-dep, and its `.cljc` suite runs on both runtimes:\n8 tests / 30 assertions, 0 failures on JVM and on nbb 1.4.210. Verified to\ndiscriminate rather than merely pass -- breaking the cljs branch of `wrap`\ntakes nbb to 4 failures and exit 1.\n\nTwo design points worth keeping:\n\n- The representation still differs by host (`long` vs `bigint`); what is\n  guaranteed is that the operations agree, including `->string` text. Boxing\n  would be paid on every arithmetic operation in a compiler hot path.\n- `clojure.core`'s predicates give WRONG ANSWERS on cljs bigint rather than\n  errors -- `(zero? (js/BigInt 0))` is false. That is why this is a namespace\n  and not a pair of constants.\n\n## Remaining candidates, with evidence\n\nNot created here, recorded so the next pass does not re-derive them:\n\n- **portable string `format`** -- 57 hits across 4 repos, no owner.\n  `kotoba-lang/fmt` is an EDN SOURCE formatter (rustfmt-equivalent), not string\n  formatting, so the name is taken and a new one is needed.\n- **`math`** -- 50 hits (amu 30, kotoba-kir 20), no owner.\n- **`java.nio` / `StandardCharsets`** -- 633 hits combined, but `fs`, `io` and\n  `bytes` already exist; this is an adoption gap, not a creation gap. Confirm\n  the decode direction is present in `bytes` before concluding otherwise.\n- **`thrown? clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo`** -- 193 hits, needs no library at\n  all: `#?(:clj clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo :cljs :default)` is the portable\n  form. A convention, not a repo.\n\n## Consequences\n\nThe four repositories can drop their private i64 spellings, which is the\nprecondition for their suites becoming `.cljc` and running on a second\nruntime. That, not the library itself, is what removes the JVM dependency.\n\n## Status note\n\n`kotoba-lang/i64` is created, pushed public, and registered in west. It has NOT\nyet been adopted by any consumer, and no fleet gate runs it. Adoption is the\nnext step and is where the measured 266 references get removed."}]
